title: 'Gnomon CI fully green on macOS, Ubuntu and Windows (draft PR #49)'
summary: |-
  The first all-green CI run (39073693925) on draft PR #49 covers:
  - tests on macos-14, ubuntu-latest and windows-latest (Node 24): contracts 125, plus kernel 170/171 on Windows with marked POSIX files excluded and registered;
  - probes on macOS and Windows;
  - native builds;
  - the Warpline guard.

  It took 8 CI rounds:
  - npm optional-deps bugs: the rolldown binding was missing, then a second `npm install --no-save` pruned the first binding;
  - Windows CRLF on byte-compared schemas;
  - Linux /tmp homes, correctly refused by the daemon;
  - Windows EPERM from fsync on a read-only handle (fixed with a writable-handle helper);
  - ESM path imports;
  - platform markers not enforced at runtime;
  - a REAL kernel-log bug: a refused open leaked its DatabaseSync handle (21 fds over 10 refused opens on macOS), fixed with an fd-count regression test;
  - one test deleting an open log's home.

  Follow-ups: T-2026-10-17-013 (docs, small) and T-014 (S34-30, a queued anchor write after a refused open can overwrite keystore evidence).
symbols_touched:
  - '#kernel-log'
  - '#gnomon-ci'
  - '#gnomon-kernel'
  - $daemon-lifecycle
commit: 874f6ace
learnings:
  - Windows CI surfaces resource leaks that POSIX hides (open files can be deleted on macOS/Linux); counting /dev/fd makes such leaks testable on macOS.
  - fsync on Windows needs a writable handle (FlushFileBuffers); 'r' handles fail with EPERM.
